-- hypermath casting.hm
-- The casting operation: □ casts through a form and returns what holds.
-- Presupposes: axioms.hm (ground, apply, relations, ax-diff, ax-sim, ax-box)
-- Status: FORM for casting definition and FORM/FRAME tripartition.
--         FRAME for conditionality-ordering chain (requires subtraction.hm).

-- ================================================================
-- § 1. Etymology — Why "casting"
-- ================================================================
--
-- cast (v.) — Old Norse kasta: to throw.
-- Two senses converge here, both structurally exact:
--
-- (1) MOLD CASTING:
--     The caster presses a mold through a material.
--     The mold is □ — the ground form, the shape that pre-exists the material.
--     The material is A — any form receiving the cast.
--     What the material retains in the shape of the mold is A'.
--     If A is perfectly □-shaped, A' == A: the mold fits exactly.
--     If A is partially □-shaped, A' ~~ A: only the similar surface holds.
--     If A has no □-receptivity, A' == absent: nothing holds.
--
-- (2) TYPE CASTING (programming):
--     cast(T, x) preserves what is T-compatible in x,
--     returns absent when x is incompatible with T.
--     Mathematicians who program carry exactly this intuition:
--     identity-preserving on compatible input; undefined otherwise.
--     No prior formal mathematics has claimed "casting" as its primary term.
--
-- Failure mode: a FRAME is a half-cast.
-- The caster went through the motions; the form did not close.
-- The spell looks real but does not hold under re-application.
-- This is the carpentry sense of "frame": the scaffolding built
-- BEFORE the wall holds. A frame becomes form or is removed.
-- It does not become form by asserting it is form.
--
-- The three structural roles of the preposition "of" in
-- "A has survived re-application of □":
--   through  — □ is the casting medium: A survives THROUGH □, not past it.
--   of       — A' belongs to □'s derivation: it is OF □'s generation.
--   because  — A' persists BECAUSE □ is the ground: not by A's own assertion.
-- These three are not collapsed into one relation.
-- They are the three structural faces of casting.

-- ================================================================
-- § 2. The Casting Operation
-- ================================================================

-- cast :: Form -> Form -> Form
--
-- cast(□, A) — □ casts through A.
-- First argument: always □ (the caster — one caster in this system).
-- Second argument: A (the form being cast through).
-- Return: A' — the form that holds after □ presses through A.

-- Three outcomes, ordered by strength of □-receptivity:

-- (a) FULL RECEPTIVITY — □ finds itself in A exactly:
--     cast(ground, A) =~ A
--     A' is congruent to A: full continuation capacity preserved.
--     The mold fits perfectly. Nothing is lost. Nothing is added.
--     This is FORM-status.
--
-- (b) PARTIAL RECEPTIVITY — □ finds similarity but not full coincidence:
--     cast(ground, A) ~~ A  additionally  not(cast(ground, A) =~ A)
--     A' is similar to A but not congruent: only the ~-surface holds.
--     The mold catches some of the material. Partial cast.
--     This is FRAME-status.
--
-- (c) NO RECEPTIVITY — rare, not the default:
--     cast(ground, A) == absent
--     A' is the absent form. Nothing of A holds under □-pressure.
--     Note: ax-sim gives similar(apply(x), ground) for all x.
--     This means EVERY form generated by □ has at least ~~ receptivity.
--     The absent outcome is reserved for forms that are not □-generated —
--     i.e., forms claimed to exist outside the □-system entirely.
--     Within the □-system: the absent outcome cannot arise from a valid form.

derive cast-totality as FORM:
    -- Every □-generated form has at least ~~ receptivity under casting.
    step 1: ax-sim: similar(apply(x), ground) for all x.
            Every form generated by apply has ~~ with ground.
    step 2: cast(ground, A) presses □ through A.
            By ax-sim, apply(A) ~~ ground.
            Therefore A shares continuation capacity with ground.
    step 3: continuation capacity overlap is preserved under the cast:
            what A shares with ground is what the cast returns.
            Since the overlap is non-empty (step 2), cast(ground, A) ~~ A
            always holds within the □-generated form space.
    step 4: the absent outcome requires zero overlap with ground.
            By step 2, this is impossible for any □-generated form.
    close: relation(~~)
    -- cast-totality: no □-generated form produces absent under casting.
    -- Every form has at least partial receptivity.

-- ================================================================
-- § 3. FORM and FRAME Derived from Casting
-- ================================================================
--
-- The FORM/FRAME distinction is now derivable from casting,
-- not merely declared as discipline in axioms.hm § 5.
-- This closes the definitional circle: FORM is not self-attested,
-- it is structurally determined by what survives cast(□, A).

derive form-status-from-casting as FORM:
    -- A derivation has FORM-status iff cast(ground, A) =~ A.
    step 1: cast(ground, A) returns A' — the form that holds.
    step 2: FORM-status requires full coincidence of continuation capacity
            between A and A': congruent(A', A) holds.
            By the definition of cast: this is cast(ground, A) =~ A.
    step 3: FORM is therefore: the form that returns itself under □-casting.
            Not the form that asserts it would return itself.
            The cast is the verification.
    step 4: self-attestation does not satisfy step 2.
            Claiming cast(ground, A) =~ A without performing the cast
            is FRAME claiming to be FORM — a kernel error.
    close: relation(=~)
    -- FORM == congruent return under □-casting.

derive frame-status-from-casting as FORM:
    -- A derivation has FRAME-status iff cast(ground, A) ~~ A
    -- additionally not(cast(ground, A) =~ A).
    step 1: cast returns A' ~~ A (partial receptivity, by cast-totality).
    step 2: the cast does not return A' =~ A: some continuation capacity is lost.
    step 3: A is articulated — it has structure.
            A is not yet closed — the cast shows the gap.
    step 4: FRAME is the half-cast: the mold pressed through,
            the ~-surface holds, but the =~-fit is absent.
    step 5: FRAME is not failure. It is truthful status.
            The gap named by FRAME is exactly what must close
            for A to achieve FORM-status.
    close: relation(~~)
    -- FRAME == partially-receptive return under □-casting,
    --          with the congruence gap explicitly named.

-- ================================================================
-- § 4. Conditionality Derived from Casting
-- ================================================================
--
-- conditionality: the gating operation on derivation space.
-- B derives only when A has achieved FORM-status under casting.
-- This is not classical material implication (P -> Q, vacuously true when P is false).
-- Classical implication is truth-functional and presupposes Boolean truth values.
-- The □-system has no Boolean truth values — it has relations.
-- □-conditionality is a structural gate, not a truth-function.

-- cond :: Form -> Form -> Form
--
-- cond(A, B):
--   if cast(ground, A) =~ A  [A is FORM]   then B
--   if cast(ground, A) ~~ A  [A is FRAME]  then absent
--
-- "absent" is not false. It is structural non-presence.
-- The derivation space does not contain B until A closes.
-- There is no result to return — not a false result, not a ⊥ error.
-- The absence is the information: B cannot derive until A has FORM.

derive conditionality-is-a-gate as FORM:
    step 1: cond(A, B) asks: does A have FORM-status?
    step 2: FORM-status is determined by cast(ground, A) =~ A (form-status-from-casting).
    step 3: if yes: B is a valid next derivation step.
            The cast confirms A holds; derivation continues to B.
    step 4: if no (A is FRAME): B does not exist yet in the derivation space.
            Not: B is false.
            Not: B is undefined.
            Meaning: B's derivation requires A to close first.
            B is waiting, not absent forever — FRAME closes, then B derives.
    step 5: cond is asymmetric: cond(A, B) != cond(B, A) in general.
            A is the precondition; B is the consequence.
            Reversing them changes which form is the gate.
    close: relation(=~)
    -- conditionality is a structural gate on the derivation space.
    -- FORM: fully derived from cast + form-status-from-casting.
    -- No classical logic borrowed.

-- ================================================================
-- § 5. The Ordering Relation (prerequisite for subtraction)
-- ================================================================
--
-- ordinatic ordering: α <= β
-- Defined from definitive-expansion (+) already in axioms.hm § 7.
-- α <= β  iff  there exists quant γ such that  α + γ == β
-- Reading: β is reachable from α by further expansion.
-- This is the ordinatic version of "less than or equal to."

derive ordinatic-ordering as FORM:
    step 1: definitive-expansion + is already FORM (axioms.hm § 7).
    step 2: define α <= β as: exists quant γ such that α + γ == β.
            γ is the expansion needed to reach β from α.
    step 3: reflexivity: α <= α because α + quanta_0 == α
            (adding the zero-expansion leaves α unchanged).
    step 4: transitivity: if α <= β and β <= δ,
            then exists γ_1 with α + γ_1 == β,
            and exists γ_2 with β + γ_2 == δ.
            Therefore α + (γ_1 + γ_2) == δ, so α <= δ.
    step 5: antisymmetry under ==: if α <= β and β <= α,
            then α == β (the only form reachable from itself by zero expansion
            that is also the origin of the other is itself).
    close: relation(==)
    -- <= is a total order on finite ordinals within the □-system.
    -- FORM: derived entirely from definitive-expansion.

-- ================================================================
-- § 7. ordinal_reflection — Ordinal Distance of the Cast Return
-- ================================================================
--
-- Etymology:
--   ordinal  — from ordo (Latin: row, order): the measurable depth
--              of a form in the □-derivation sequence. Its position label.
--   reflect  — from re- (back) + flectere (to bend): to bend back toward origin.
--              To see the starting form from the return position.
--   ordinal_reflection: the ordinal distance between the starting form A
--   and the form A' that returns after cast(□, A) completes one full cycle.
--   It measures how far the cast bent the form away from its origin.
--
-- ordinal_reflection :: Form -> Form -> Relation
--
-- Takes two Forms (A and A') directly — not Links.
-- Links are defined in links.hm, which is the layer after this one.
-- ordinal_reflection is defined here on Forms because the cast produces Forms.
-- links.hm applies ordinal_reflection to the specific form pairs
-- that arise from link traversals (start form and return form).
--
-- UNCONDITIONAL: does not invoke cond. Operates on Forms as forms.
-- Returns the relation classifying the ordinal distance between A and A'.
--
-- Three outcomes:
--
--   == (zero ordinal distance):
--      A' is at the same ordinal position as A, by the same derivation path.
--      The cycle closed back to exact identity. Commutative.
--
--   =~ (zero continuation distance, non-zero path distance):
--      A' has the same continuation futures as A but arrived by a different path.
--      Structurally commutative (same futures), path-distinct.
--
--   ~~ (non-zero ordinal distance):
--      A' is in the ~~-class of A but at a different ordinal position.
--      Non-commutative. Orientation was changed by the cycle.
--      This is the default case (Möbius) for non-trivial casts.

derive ordinal_reflection-is-unconditional as FORM:
    step 1: ordinal_reflection(A, A') measures the relation between
            A (the starting form) and A' (the cast return form).
    step 2: this measurement is a comparison of ordinal positions.
            Ordinal positions are forms (axioms.hm primitives).
            Comparing forms requires only the three relations (~~, =~, ==)
            which are defined for all forms without preconditions.
    step 3: no cond gate is required.
            cond(A, B) gates derivation steps.
            ordinal_reflection is not a derivation step — it is a measurement.
            Measurements in the □-system use the relations directly.
    step 4: when A' comes from a FRAME form (cast partially closed):
            ordinal_reflection returns ~~ at most.
            A FRAME form has partial continuation capacity (~~) but not full (=~).
            Measuring against partial capacity yields only partial reflection.
            When A' achieves FORM, ordinal_reflection is re-evaluated
            and may promote to =~ or ==.
    close: relation(=~)
    -- ordinal_reflection is unconditional and total over all Form pairs.
    -- FORM at =~.

derive ordinal_reflection-is-symmetric as FORM:
    -- ordinal_reflection(A, A') == ordinal_reflection(A', A)
    step 1: ordinal_reflection measures ordinal distance between A and A'.
    step 2: ordinal distance is not directed: the distance from A to A'
            is the same quantity as the distance from A' to A.
            The measurement does not carry the orientation of the cast —
            it measures the gap, not the direction of the gap.
    step 3: therefore swapping A and A' does not change the distance measured.
            ordinal_reflection(A, A') == ordinal_reflection(A', A).
    step 4: this is required for ordinal_reflection to be a valid instrument:
            if the instrument were orientation-sensitive, it would import
            the asymmetry it measures into itself.
            A measuring tool must not inherit what it measures.
    close: relation(==)
    -- ordinal_reflection is symmetric. FORM at ==.

-- ================================================================
-- § 7. Open FRAME — Subtraction (requires this file to close)
-- ================================================================
--
-- ordinatic-subtraction: α ⊖ β
-- FRAME: defined in structure, not yet written as a derive block.
--
-- Definition shape:
--   α ⊖ β  ==  cond(β <= α, the quant γ such that β + γ == α)
--
-- What this requires:
--   (a) cond — FORM in this file (§ 4 above).
--   (b) ordinatic-ordering <= — FORM in this file (§ 5 above).
--   (c) the existence of γ — requires ordinatic arithmetic to guarantee
--       existence within the finite segment (α, β < ordinal_limit).
--
-- Finite-layer totality claim (to be proven in subtraction.hm):
--   For all α, β < ordinal_limit: if β <= α then exists unique γ < ordinal_limit
--   such that β + γ == α.
--   This is classical natural number subtraction recovered at the finite layer.
--   It does not hold at transfinite depth (ordinal_limit ⊖ quanta_1 is absent
--   because no γ exists such that quanta_1 + γ == ordinal_limit).
--   At transfinite depth: route through W (wrap operation, Chapter 50)
--   before applying subtraction.
--
-- Closure criterion: subtraction.hm writes the derive block with full existence proof.
-- FRAME until subtraction.hm is written.
